    The interpreter's fixed-width arithmetic natives raise IntegerOverflow

    Climb batch 6b, rung O (rows 379, 427). Under walk the ten signed natives
    (Negate, Add, Sub, Mul and Div of Int and Long) and the eight unsigned ones
    (Negate, Add, Sub and Mul of NN32 and UnsignedLong) raise the catchable
    IntegerOverflow where the result does not fit, as the specification says
    and the compiled path already does; row 379's expected failure is the plain
    test FixedWidthOverflowRungB, intPrim and longPrim assert the raise, and
    the demo HeapShakedown's spread is respelled with the wrapping operators.
    Of 413 interpreter tests none changed. The reserved stop "a library body
    found to rely on wrapping" was met at three range bodies, lifted as
    reversible and listed; rows 449-453 are opened, and rows 450-453 are gated
    by five expected failures.

    Folded at the gather: REPORT.md written from the repair round's structured
    result, the second skeptic's three corrections made (the reach of
    RangeInternals.fss:989 on descending unsigned ranges, XXXRangeEmptyHashRungO
    for MIN # 0, an NN32 assertion in XXXRangeSizeZZ64RungO), rows 379 and 427
    closed, notes appended to rows 403, 326, 325, 315 and 453.

    historical: ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/interpreter/glue/prim/Int.java, Long.java, NN32.java, UnsignedLong.java, ProjectFortress/tests/intPrim.fss, ProjectFortress/tests/longPrim.fss, ProjectFortress/demos/HeapShakedown.fss. The renamed XXXFixedWidthOverflowRungB.fss and the new RungO tests are the revival's own.

    Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AmiXNpJxQ6TBwec4vJZHDB

